<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="TCA cycle II (plants and fungi)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5690" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; The TCA pathway is a catabolic pathway of mostly aerobic respiration that generates both energy and reducing power. In addition, it is also the first step in generating precursors for biosynthesis. The pathway is very common, and a variation of it exists in practically all aerobic living organisms |CITS: [16746397][16746585][16747930]| as well as some anaerobic bacteria |CITS: [3049083][11131021]|. The input to the cycle is |FRAME: ACETYL-COA|, an activated form of |FRAME: ACET| that is generated by the degradation of carbohydrates, fats and proteins. A common source of acetyl-coA is |FRAME: PYRUVATE|, which is generated by |FRAME: GLYCOLYSIS glycolysis| and converted to acetyl-CoA by the |FRAME: PYRUVDEHYD-PWY &quot;pyruvate dehydrogenase complex&quot;|. In every turn this variant of the TCA cycle converts one molecule of acetyl-CoA into two |FRAME: CARBON-DIOXIDE| molecules, reduces a total of four molecules of either |FRAME: NAD|, |FRAME: NADP|, or |FRAME: Quinones quinone| to |FRAME: NADH|, |FRAME: NADPH| and |FRAME: Reduced-Quinones quinol|, respectively, and phosphorylates one molecule of nucleotide diphosphate (ADP or GDP) to nucleotide triphosphate (ATP or GTP). The reduced molecules of NADH/NADPH/quinol that are formed by the TCA cycle serve as electron donors for oxidative phosphorylation (see for example |FRAME: PWY-3781|). In that process the electrons flow to a terminal acceptor, powering on their way proton pumps that transport protons across the cytoplasmic or mitochondrial membranes, generating a proton motive force (PMF). As the protons return to their original location, they power ATPase enzymes that phosphorylate ADP molecules to ATP. The total energy gained from the complete breakdown of one molecule of glucose by glycolysis, the TCA cycle, and oxidative phosphorylation equals about 30 ATP molecules in eukaryotes. The name of the TCA (short for tricarboxylic acid) cycle is derived from the fact that the first step in the pathway is attachment of acetyl-coA to |FRAME: CIT|, an acid with three carboxylate groups. The pathway is also known as the citric acid cycle, and as the Szent-Gyorgyi-Krebs cycle (or just the Krebs cycle), named after the scientists who described it. Some organisms possess a truncated version of the TCA cycle, known as the |FRAME: GLYOXYLATE-BYPASS &quot;glyoxylate cycle&quot;|, that converts |FRAME:ACETYL-COA| to biosynthetic intermediates without the loss of |FRAME: CARBON-DIOXIDE| |CITS:[6378912][3333001][3332993]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; One of the main differences between the eukaryotic TCA cycles (described in this pathway and in |FRAME: PWY66-398|) and the |FRAME: TCA &quot;prokaryotic pathway&quot;| is that the latter contains an alternative route between |FRAME: MAL| and |FRAME: OXALACETIC_ACID|, catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-1.1.5.4|. That enzyme is found in some bacteria, but is absent from most eukaryotes, in which the reaction is catalyzed only by the |FRAME: EC-1.1.1.37 &quot;NAD+-dependent malate dehydrogenase, EC 1.1.1.37&quot;|. Another difference concerns the enzyme isocitrate dehydrogenase. Most prokaryotes possess only an NADP+-dependent form (|FRAME: EC-1.1.1.42|), which supports both respiration via the TCA cycle and glutamate biosynthesis for anabolic reactions. Eukaryotes, on the other hand, have two types of the enzyme - an NADP+-dependent forms (EC 1.1.1.42) and NAD+-dependent forms (EC 1.1.1.41). It has been shown that the NADP+-dependent enzymes are involved primarily in glutamate biosynthesis, while the NAD+-dependent enzymes are those involved in the TCA cycle |CITS: [2198251]|. It should be noted that while rare, NAD+-dependent isocitrate dehydrogenases are also found in bacteria that lack the |FRAME: GLYOXYLATE-BYPASS &quot;glyoxylate bypass&quot;|, and thus are missing the enzyme |FRAME: ISOCIT-CLEAV-RXN &quot;isocitrate lyase&quot;| |CITS: [15653464]|. Examples for prokaryotes that contain an NAD-dependent enzyme include |FRAME: TAX-930| |CITS: [12204383]| and |FRAME: TAX-542| |CITS: [22117777]|. The only difference between this pathway, found in plants and fungi, and |FRAME: PWY66-398|, which is found in animals, is the step between |FRAME: SUC-COA| and |FRAME: SUC|. While plants and fungi only use |FRAME: EC-6.2.1.5|, animals also use |FRAME: EC-6.2.1.4|.</Description>
